<h2>The case in brief</h2>
<p>The petitioner stood accused in FIR No. 60/2026 of Police Station Jawahar Nagar, Sri Ganganagar, for offences under Sections 115(2), 118(2), 110, 126(2), 191(1)(3) and 190 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita — allegations that the complainant was assaulted with a sword and an iron rod. The application for regular bail was made under Section 483 of the BNSS (the successor to Section 439 CrPC), before Justice Chandra Shekhar Sharma at the Jodhpur seat.</p>

<h2>What each side argued</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>For the petitioner</h3><p>The allegations were omnibus, custody had been long, and — centrally — three co-accused had already been granted bail by coordinate benches, in orders of 28 April 2026 and 2 July 2026. Parity was claimed.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>For the State</h3><p>Two of the injuries were grievous. But the prosecution's own description placed them on the arm and the leg, with the remaining injuries simple and none on a vital part of the body.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The Court, considering the rival submissions and the totality of the facts and circumstances, was inclined to enlarge the petitioner on bail — expressly without any opinion on the merits — and allowed the application on a bond of Rs 50,000 with two sureties of Rs 25,000 each, conditioned on appearance at every hearing until the trial ends.</p>

<h2>Reading a routine order</h2>
<p>The order does not announce which factor carried the day, and that reticence is itself instructive. Three features of the record did the work that long paragraphs do in reported judgments.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Parity</h3><p>Three co-accused in the same FIR were already out on coordinate-bench orders — the strongest structural argument available.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Injury profile</h3><p>Grievous injuries existed, but to limbs, not vital parts — a distinction prosecutors themselves are obliged to state accurately.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>No merits findings</h3><p>The Court said nothing about guilt, keeping the trial court entirely free — the hallmark of a properly drawn bail order.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For bail practice:</strong> when co-accused have been released, obtain and annex those orders — with dates, application numbers and the roles ascribed to each accused — so the court can see the similarity rather than take counsel's word for it. And engage with the medico-legal record precisely: where grievous injuries are to non-vital parts, saying so accurately is more persuasive than minimising them.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>A grant of bail "without expressing any opinion on the merits" is not a comment on innocence, and conditions are not formalities: the bond, sureties and appearance requirements are the price of liberty pending trial, and their breach invites cancellation under the same Section 483 BNSS jurisdiction that granted the relief.</p>
</div>
